WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.040
- Hi, It's Dina.

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.980
I'm here with Queer Eye's Tan France

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:04.077
and we're going to have a conversation

00:00:04.077 --> 00:00:06.610
about representation on telly.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.290
(atmospheric electronic music)

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.630
Do you remember the first time

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:14.500
you ever saw yourself represented
on British television.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.230
- It was not when I was younger,

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.380
it was just a little bit younger, 2018.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.440
It was when I represented myself.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.030
There was nobody else when I was younger

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.460
that represented people like me.

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.960
I never saw a gay Asian on TV before.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.780
We were not the romantic
interest, we were not the lead.

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:33.450
We were not the person
who was seen as desirable.

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.070
We were not the person
who was seen as the person

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.690
everybody wanted to get,
that wasn't our role.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.190
- Did you feel a certain pressure

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.050
knowing that you were stepping
into this role for people.

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.410
- Yeah, it's the reason
why I at first didn't think

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.030
I wanted to audition for the show.

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.740
My husband was actually
the one who convinced me.

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:51.877
He said, "You've been
talking about this for many,

00:00:51.877 --> 00:00:53.827
"many years that you've
never seen a version of you

00:00:53.827 --> 00:00:56.197
"on screen before, and
now this opportunity's

00:00:56.197 --> 00:01:00.177
"presented itself and you're
choosing to turn it down.

00:01:00.177 --> 00:01:02.677
"You get to be that version

00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:05.350
"of the person you so
desperately craved as a child."

00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:07.490
- Why do you think it's important

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.620
to have representation on screen?

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:12.690
- Not seeing a version of myself on TV

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:17.030
meant that I wasn't, we weren't,

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.150
my people weren't normalized.

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:20.750
I felt like I was alone.

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.960
I felt like the life I was
living wasn't appropriate,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.240
that it could be judged.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.900
When you see representation in the media

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.070
of those minority groups it
means it's somewhat normalized.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:35.410
People are starting to understand
not just the stereotype,

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:38.030
not just the caricature of that person,

00:01:38.030 --> 00:01:40.150
what they truly represent,
they're seen as humans.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.760
They're seen as real people.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.350
So that's what I think
representation means.

00:01:44.350 --> 00:01:46.920
That's what representation
brings to a community

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.940
is the sense of normalcy, the
sense that they do belong.

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.930
- How far do you think that
the entertainment industry

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:54.720
has come in terms of representation

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.470
from when you were a kid in the UK?

00:01:58.539 --> 00:02:00.530
- It's come a relatively long way.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:01.420
- Right.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:03.770
- You will see more often

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:09.210
a slightly better mix of ethnicities on TV

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.240
or people who are gay
or people who are trans.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.210
So a broader range of representation,

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:18.083
but I still don't feel like
it's where it needs to be.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:22.740
As I said, I'm still one of
the very first to do this,

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:26.560
and it's not like I'm some
obscure race here in the UK.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.070
There's a huge percentage of
the population here in the UK

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:32.200
that is Pakistani, there is
a huge population within that

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:34.410
of Asians in general who are also gay,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:36.950
yet that is not represented on TV.

00:02:36.950 --> 00:02:40.213
So yes, we've come a long
way in certain areas.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.940
It's nowhere where it needs to be still.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:47.010
- How often do you have
this kind of conversation?

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.620
Is it something that, do
you get a fatigue sometimes

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.640
having to chat about it?
- No.

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:52.500
- No, okay.
- No.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:53.770
- How come?

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:58.360
- Because I know why I
took this job on Queer Eye.

00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:00.490
I wanna do a cute makeover,
don't get me wrong.

00:03:00.490 --> 00:03:02.000
It's really fun to play with clothes.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.070
However, I had an agenda
when I took this job.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:08.620
I wanted to be seen, I
wanted people to see me.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.570
I wanted somebody to
have the balls to say,

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:12.280
you are different and
you don't belong on TV

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:13.400
because you're different.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.080
Now I need you to see
me for everything I am.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:17.550
And so if it means that people

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.010
have to hear this conversation over

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:20.790
and over again and they are sick of it,

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:23.790
I don't care if you're
sick of this conversation,

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:24.623
you need to hear it.

00:03:24.623 --> 00:03:26.000
If you're sick of this conversation,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.050
you're the person who needs to hear this.

